<DocsLayout title="Full SwiftUI App" section="examples">
  <h1>Full SwiftUI App</h1>
  <p class="text-lg text-muted-foreground mt-1 mb-8">A complete profile screen with async loading, error handling, and proper architecture.</p>

  <h2>Model</h2>
  <p>A shared <code>User</code> struct used across the app.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={modelCode} title="User.swift" />

  <h2>Protocols</h2>
  <p>Contracts for the network layer and user repository. Each layer only knows about the protocol above it.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={protocolsCode} title="Protocols.swift" />

  <h2>Network Service</h2>
  <p>A thin wrapper around <code>URLSession</code> that handles JSON decoding.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={networkServiceCode} title="NetworkService.swift" />

  <h2>User Repository</h2>
  <p>Takes <code>NetworkServiceProtocol</code> as a constructor dependency. The container provides it at registration time.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={userRepositoryCode} title="UserRepository.swift" />

  <h2>Registration</h2>
  <p>An <code>AppDependencies</code> struct registers both services with the <code>.singleton</code> policy. The <code>container</code> parameter in the factory closure resolves previously registered dependencies.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={registrationCode} title="AppDependencies.swift" />
  <CodeBlock code={appEntryCode} title="TaskFlowApp.swift" />

  <h2>ViewModel</h2>
  <p><code>@Injectable</code> generates an <code>init</code> with <code>userRepository</code> defaulted from the container. The view model never knows about <code>NetworkService</code> &mdash; that detail is hidden behind the repository protocol. In tests, pass a mock repository directly to the init.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={viewModelCode} title="ProfileViewModel.swift" />

  <h2>View</h2>
  <p>A standard SwiftUI view observing the view model. No awareness of the DI layer.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={viewCode} title="ProfileView.swift" />

